<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  .what = a SEAT-SCOPED imagemagick policy. it closes the coder classes that
          hand a file to another program or interpret it as a program, and it
          leaves every raster format this repo actually renders untouched.

  .why  = `4.5.nvim` installs imagemagick, because image.nvim converts an image
          in the tree nvim has open. that tree is written by `git.grove.pull`,
          which is bytes a GROVE named — and a grove is ASSUMED COMPROMISED.

          the debian default at `/etc/ImageMagick-6/policy.xml` describes ITSELF
          as an "open security policy" and warns in its own comment that it
          "might present security vulnerabilities in less regulated conditions".
          it closes `path @*` and the URL/HTTPS/HTTP delegates, and declares NO
          coder rule at all. an absent coder rule is a PERMITTED coder rule.

          measured on this box 2026-08-31, imagemagick 6.9.12:
            ghostscript PRESENT at /usr/bin/gs, /usr/bin/ghostscript
            a benign .eps read as coder PS — the ghostscript reach is live
            12 of the 16 coders below carried by the build, all open

  .where = `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/ImageMagick/policy.xml`

  ⚠️ .why the SEAT path and not /etc
       measured 2026-08-31: a policy at the seat path BITES with no env var
       that names it, on both the `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` route and the
       `$HOME/.config` fallback. `identify -list policy` reports it as a second
       `Path:` beside `/etc/ImageMagick-6/policy.xml`.

       so the holder needs NO root — which matters because the CAMPER seat is
       the seat that runs the editor and holds no sudo. a box-wide write would
       have to be owned by `ground`, and every camper would then depend on a
       fact only the other seat could set (rule.require.seam-claims-have-an-owner).

  ⚠️ .this file is COPIED, never symlinked
       so `configure.verify` can `cmp` the installed copy against this checkout
       and catch a stale one (rule.require.repo-as-source-of-truth).
-->
<policymap>

  <!--
    1. the GHOSTSCRIPT class.
       PS/PS2/PS3/EPS/PDF/XPS are not decoded in-process — imagemagick execs
       `gs` and hands it the file. so a coder rule here is the difference
       between "a grove chose some bytes" and "a grove chose the input to an
       interpreter that has had remote-code-execution CVEs of its own".
  -->
  <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{PS,PS2,PS3,EPS,PDF,XPS}" />

  <!--
    2. the INTERPRETED class.
       MVG and MSL are not image formats at all — they are draw/automation
       languages that imagemagick executes. MSL is XML with file read and write
       verbs. this is the ImageTragick class.

       ⚠️ MAGICK is here because it is the pseudo-coder that lets a file name
          its own coder, which would re-open every line above.
  -->
  <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{MSL,MVG,MAGICK}" />

  <!--
    3. the LOCAL-READ / LOCAL-EXEC class.
       TEXT and PLT read a local path. SHOW and WIN spawn a display program.
       LABEL and CAPTION render text through a font stack. EPHEMERAL DELETES
       its input after read, which is a write primitive dressed as a decoder.

       none of the six formats `src/init.lua` declares renderable
       (png jpg jpeg gif webp avif) reaches any of these.
  -->
  <policy domain="coder" rights="none" pattern="{TEXT,SHOW,WIN,PLT,LABEL,CAPTION,EPHEMERAL}" />

  <!--
    4. every DELEGATE.
       a delegate is a line in `delegates.xml` that execs another program.
       the debian default closes three (URL, HTTPS, HTTP) and leaves the rest.

       ⚠️ MEASURED to cost zero here: all six renderable formats still read
          with this line in force. if a box's build routes one of them through
          a delegate, `configure.verify`'s cost claim goes ✋ and names this
          line — the verify asks BOTH halves, so a too-wide policy is caught
          rather than discovered later as a broken image render.
  -->
  <policy domain="delegate" rights="none" pattern="*" />

</policymap>
